# Deploy Workflow

This project uses Vercel connected to GitHub. Pushing to `main` deploys to production. Pushing to any other branch deploys to a Vercel preview URL only.

## Default behavior — always give preview commands first

When the user asks to commit, push, or deploy changes, give commands for a **preview branch only**. Never include `git push origin main` unless the user explicitly asks to go to production.

Default preview commands to give:

```bash
git checkout -b <descriptive-branch-name>   # or git checkout <branch> if it already exists
git add <specific files>
git commit -m "your commit message"
git push origin <branch-name>
```

After giving these commands, add a note like:
> ✅ This pushes to **preview only**. Vercel will deploy a preview URL — test it there first.
> When you're ready to go to production, say "push to prod" and I'll give you the merge command.

## Branch naming

Use short, descriptive kebab-case names based on what the feature or fix is. Examples:
- `draft-mode`
- `unsaved-changes-modal`
- `copy-signup`
- `fix/date-display`

## Production — only when explicitly requested

Only give production commands when the user says something like:
- "push to prod"
- "it's ready, deploy to production"
- "merge to main"
- "ship it"

When that happens, give:

```bash
git checkout main
git pull origin main
git merge <branch-name>
git push origin main
git branch -d <branch-name>
```

And add a note:
> 🚀 This merges to `main` and deploys to **production**.

## Important rules

- Never combine preview and production commands in the same response
- Never suggest `git push origin main` as the default push command
- If the user is already on a non-main branch, just use `git push origin <current-branch>` — no need to create a new one
- If the user is on `main` with uncommitted changes, always move them to a new branch before pushing
